86045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119928 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1045 "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120047 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "dcdc8ecc-c5d8-56ba-b664-0870beb832b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449263 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186120153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449264 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186120153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449265 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186120153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449266 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186120153 #edb/ref 1069 "q-156719" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120153 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120153 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x-y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- **II.** $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- **III.** $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **IV.** $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186120153 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ceae9baf-b635-5e21-b116-a9e3e8cfea9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120153 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x-y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y \\\\ (0) & =(0)-(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}-{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}-{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})-({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x-y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}-{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}-k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x-y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1061 41645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f009ee8-4a5e-5da7-b539-b2991cbd9d04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120884 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1061 41646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a7eb434d-3210-5f2f-b5ec-18781d0fa02d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120885 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1061 41660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "911d42c3-5fd0-515e-abcd-cfaab6aa6926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120886 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120887 #edb/ref 1061 41667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120887 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b4b4898-6c15-52d9-96fe-d57251b08369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120887 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1061 41674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "124c571f-28f0-56fa-a68d-76ef241f4618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120888 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1061 41675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d8c8e20-696b-5df3-beaf-a78962ce0420" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120889 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1064 "A **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^n$ that is closed  under vector addition and scalar multiplication. In other words: \n\n$H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $L$ with equation $y=3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$ \n\nFirst, notice that we can express the points on $L$ as the following set:\n\n![](images/1f/1f380f9928da2dd44ff157a72fce97c02ae44626df515d52ece6ff5feee302d2.svg)\n\nIn other words, $L$ contains all vectors of the form ![](images/90/90996c2ffae29c3bd9cd3ab398cca4dd7e1b8fbe275de8520a72a16e504fb0f3.svg) where $x$ is a real number.\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/59/59be22e80d0be32c84a8c2bdb2f2c96b23a0941d4aba26bdefa078fd299c137c.svg) and ![](images/d7/d78ea4fcfbd469e88b7fe46e28ac6f396a538c577f4defdd1b3d60e60df3c4b7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}+3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/75/759c7883ad2772f20a090ad2f8c5dfe01031ca31e2b5f0e7681c945c9256c91d.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n**Note:** Since the second condition must be satisfied for any $c,$ including $c=0,$ we can conclude that every subspace must contain the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$ So, if a set does not contain $\\mathbf{0}$ then it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor instance, the set \n\n![](images/a1/a15e0b4fc4544421e572d15b8fd016c3fa8721e0ecf3f8808a469c3252d1f6d6.svg)\n\ndoes *not* contain the zero vector (since $0 \\neq 0 + 1$), and consequently it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1065 6853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ee2c580c-5386-5e06-9b58-e9e1dd398cd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121126 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n*Closure under vector addition:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n*Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nLet $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,1,-2).$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ \n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n![](images/25/25fa34994b33e0d11fd8990dddbfda276857e699018b4701c6e2eaf13acb0c20.svg)\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace, we need to check the two conditions above:\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/d7/d7f1a3cf1625ff12bc96324dc428e1c95d271d8a72de603b671f722b9173281d.svg) and ![](images/25/255fc61225b017a51ae60b1d157787ac4b60fe596ebaa8296b48053b87b2eae7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ -2{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}+4{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2{u}_{1})+(-2{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1}) \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/7f/7f7df4272eae1975b5802d54dfae499c528661ba0ae82faff45bb26f6cfab276.svg) Then, for any scalar $c,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1065 6859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fb0bb214-7f56-557a-82f7-baeef815457e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121127 #edb/ref 1067 "Lines as Subspaces of Three-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1064 "Again, recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nConsider the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $z=4x-2y$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](images/95/956757aa0ce5b4721db582cd9d03dffaa21b1fdf6f8c60b5c6465f9cf012201d.svg)\n\nIs $\\Pi$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ To find out, we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $\\Pi,$ namely, ![](images/da/da46ac38012b220c5cf956076a81b76a21eb1242d21fae69f071531401d5d41d.svg) and ![](images/3e/3ee33188044335cd84f42b7621a5d255fdab972d89bef3d816c2b5a4da93dab0.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}+4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})-2\\cdot ({u}_{2}+{v}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $\\Pi,$ say, ![](images/27/277ea18a8e1721fdaa880025e3abb4fcb1ff3e8ec96730ca7597fbd584ef6500.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot c{u}_{1}-2\\cdot c{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $\\Pi$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1065 6860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3fcbfc6c-e5f6-5344-8ddd-779b625a03ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121128 #edb/ref 1067 "Planes as Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465169 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465170 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465171 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465172 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275390 #edb/ref 1069 "q-157300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275390 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275390 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(2,1,-1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **IV.** $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275390 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "712144d5-1b91-55e7-970d-beb256e92d9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275390 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2a+2b \\\\ a+b \\\\ (-a)+(-b)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2(a+b) \\\\ a+b \\\\ -(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k(2a) \\\\ ka \\\\ k(-a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2(ka) \\\\ ka \\\\ -(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275396 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465193 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275396 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465194 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275396 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465195 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275396 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465196 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275396 #edb/ref 1069 "q-157464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275396 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275396 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x+2y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- **II.** $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- **III.** $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **IV.** $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275396 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19a5b6b6-a775-5cf8-81ee-dfec54297bbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275396 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x+2y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x+2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+2y \\\\ (0) & =(0)+2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+2{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})+2({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}+2(k{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186417212 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417212 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417212 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417212 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218641